## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic data structures beyond arrays, focusing on a Trie with counters to support prefix queries and duplicates, a common interview topic.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node counters
- Path updates for insert/remove
- Prefix query via traverse returning passCount
- Edge cases: removing non-existent words and zero counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next=new Map(); this.pass=0; this.end=0; } }
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  addWord(w){ let node=this.root; node.pass++; for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.pass++; } node.end++; }
  removeWord(w){ let stack=[]; let node=this.root; stack.push(node); for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return; node=node.next.get(ch); stack.push(node); } if(stack[stack.length-1].end===0) return; stack.forEach(n=>n.pass--); stack[stack.length-1].end--; }
  countPrefix(p){ let node=this.root; for(const ch of p){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node=node.next.get(ch); } return node.pass; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle memory usage for large alphabets or dynamic languages?
- How would you extend to support deletion of a specific instance when duplicates exist and maintain performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a prefix-based data structure with duplicates and two complementary operations: prefix aggregation and k-th element selection within a constrained subtree.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmented with passCount and endCount per node
- Duplicate handling via endCount
- Prefix-range query via passCount at prefix node
- K-th retrieval via lexicographic traversal using child passCounts

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.pass = 0;
    this.end = 0;
  }
}
class TrieDict {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let p = this.root; p.pass++;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!p.children.has(ch)) p.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      p = p.children.get(ch); p.pass++;
    }
    p.end++;
  }
  erase(word) {
    if (!this._exists(word)) return;
    let p = this.root; p.pass--;
    for (const ch of word) {
      const nxt = p.children.get(ch); nxt.pass--;
      if (nxt.pass === 0) p.children.delete(ch); else p = nxt;
    }
    p.end--;
  }
  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    const node = this._getNode(prefix); return node ? node.pass : 0;
  }
  kthWordWithPrefix(prefix, k) {
    const node = this._getNode(prefix); if (!node) return null;
    let res = prefix; let cur = node;
    // If k falls on words exactly equal to prefix
    if (cur.end >= k) return res;
    k -= cur.end;
    while (true) {
      const keys = Array.from(cur.children.keys()).sort();
      for (const c of keys) {
        const child = cur.children.get(c);
        if (child.pass >= k) { res += c; cur = child; if (cur.end >= k) return res; k -= cur.end; break; }
        k -= child.pass;
      } else { return null; }
    }
  }
  _getNode(s) {
    let p = this.root; for (const ch of s) { if (!p.children.has(ch)) return null; p = p.children.get(ch); } return p;
  }
  _exists(word) { const n = this._getNode(word); return !!n && n.end > 0; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for memory-time trade-offs if alphabet size is large (e.g., Unicode)?
- How would you extend to support delete-on-zero-branch cleanup without amortized complexity spikes?

## Why This Is Asked
Maintains compact histogram with dynamic quantile queries in O(log D).

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree for prefix counts over fixed domain
- Lower_bound on Fenwick to locate quantile index
- Edge handling when total is zero or p at bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
class HistogramQuantile {
  constructor(){
    this.N = 2001;
    this.ft = new Array(this.N+1).fill(0);
  }
  update(i, delta){
    for (let x = i; x <= this.N; x += x & -x) this.ft[x] += delta;
  }
  insertValue(v){ this.update(v + 1001, 1); }
  eraseValue(v){ this.update(v + 1001, -1); }
  sum(i){ let s = 0; for (let x = i; x > 0; x -= x & -x) s += this.ft[x]; return s; }
  lowerBound(k){
    let idx = 0, bit = 1 << 11; // > 2001
    let acc = 0;
    for (; bit; bit >>= 1){
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.N && acc + this.ft[next] < k){ idx = next; acc += this.ft[next]; }
    }
    return idx + 1;
  }
  findQuantile(p){
    const total = this.sum(this.N);
    if (total === 0) return null;
    const k = Math.ceil(p * total);
    const i = this.lowerBound(k);
    return i - 1001;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support a dynamic domain or streaming removal without totalCount updates?
- Analyze memory and cache behavior for frequent updates.
